CM Off-centre-fed dipole (Windom): 41.1 m of 2 mm wire, 10 m above average ground.
CM Fed 13.79 m from one end, about a third along, which is the whole point of the design:
CM there the feed sits near 130 ohms on 40 m instead of a few thousand, so a 4:1 balun
CM brings it within reach of the radio on several bands from one wire.
CM This model gives 131 - j24 ohms at 7.1 MHz. The balun itself is NOT modelled here -
CM NEC-2 reports the antenna's own feed impedance and knows nothing of what is bolted to
CM it. Read the SWR against 200 ohms and you have what the radio sees through an ideal
CM 4:1: about 1.6 to 1. Against 50 ohms, the antenna alone is 2.7 to 1.
CM Try 10.1 or 21.2 MHz to see why an OCF skips those bands: the feed lands on a
CM current minimum there and the impedance runs to thousands of ohms.
CM The pattern card sweeps the whole upper hemisphere, so the plots cut through the
CM main lobe: at 7.1 MHz the wire is about a wavelength long and fires off its corners
CM rather than straight out sideways.
CM EMWS example by ZR1JT. CC0 1.0 - public domain.
